// Cs12.grammar — C# 12.0: primary constructors for classes (T3.12.1).
//
// A primary constructor for a class is a class declaration with a parameter list (like a record's
// positional parameter list). The parameters are in scope in the class body:
//   class MyClass(int x, string y)
//   {
//       // x and y are available here
//   }
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Parser/LanguageParser.cs):
//   * ParseMainTypeDeclaration (1789): the common parser for class/struct/interface/record/extension.
//   * name = ParseIdentifierToken() (1821); typeParameters = ParseTypeParameterList() (1824).
//   * paramList (1827-1828): `CurrentToken.Kind == OpenParenToken || isExtension
//     ? ParseParenthesizedParameterList(...) : null` — the parameter list is present only when "("
//     follows the (type-parameter) name. For a class this is the PRIMARY CONSTRUCTOR.
//   * baseList (1830): ParseBaseList() (the ": Base" list).
//   * body (1847-1914): `;` (no body) OR `{` members* `}` with an optional trailing `;`.
//   * constructTypeDeclaration (1965-2078), ClassKeyword case (1974-1988): builds ClassDeclaration
//     with the paramList slot (1982) — the primary constructor.
//   * ParseParenthesizedParameterList (4750): requireOneElement:false (an empty "()" is allowed).
//   * Version gate — a class primary constructor is a C# 12 feature (binder/semantic). The PARSER
//     accepts the form wherever the rule exists; version purity comes from the rule being Cs12-only.
//
// T0.3 merge semantics: re-declaring ClassDeclaration here APPENDS a new alternative to the Cs1
// ClassDeclaration (Cs1.grammar:34) and the Cs2 ClassDeclaration (Cs2.grammar:67). The new alternative
// REQUIRES the ClassPrimaryConstructorParameterList (REQUIRED-new-construct), so it is MUTUALLY
// EXCLUSIVE with both:
//   * Cs1 (no param list): for `class C(int x) { }` the Cs1 alternative sees "(" where it expects a
//     BaseList (":") or ClassBody ("{") and FAILS; only the Cs12 alternative matches. No tie.
//   * Cs2 (REQUIRED TypeParameterList): for `class C(int x) { }` the Cs2 alternative sees "(" where
//     it expects "<" and FAILS. No tie.
//   * The greedy merged TypeName (Cs2.grammar:44) consumes a generic name (`class C<T>(int x) { }`
//     -> TypeName = `C<T>`), so the generic primary-constructor form is handled without an explicit
//     TypeParameterList here (Boundary decision D1, Cs2.grammar:16-28).
// The TypeDeclaration union (Cs1.grammar:27-32) references ClassDeclaration by name, so it picks up
// the new alternative automatically (no need to re-declare TypeDeclaration, unlike the Cs9 record).
//
// CS12 only: at v<=11 the Cs12 ClassDeclaration alternative is ABSENT, so `class C(int x) { }` leaves
// the "(...)" unconsumed and REJECTS.
//
// ClassPrimaryConstructorParameterList = "(" (Parameter,)* ")" — empty allowed (`class C() { }`).
// Reuses Cs1 Parameter (Cs1.grammar:472). A dedicated rule (not reusing Cs9 RecordParameterList) keeps
// this file's dependencies confined to Cs1 (consistent with the inlining pattern in Cs11.grammar).

// === Class declaration with a primary constructor (C# 12.0). ===
// Roslyn ParseMainTypeDeclaration paramList (LanguageParser.cs:1827-1828) + ClassKeyword case
// (1974-1988). The parameter list sits between the (greedy) TypeName and the optional BaseList,
// exactly as in Roslyn. The body reuses Cs1 ClassBody (Cs1.grammar:102).
ClassDeclaration = Attributes? ClassModifier* "class" TypeName ClassPrimaryConstructorParameterList BaseList? ClassBody ";"?;

// The class primary-constructor parameter list: "(" (param,)* ")" — empty allowed (`class C() { }`).
// Roslyn ParseParenthesizedParameterList (LanguageParser.cs:4750), requireOneElement:false. Reuses
// Cs1 Parameter (Cs1.grammar:472).
ClassPrimaryConstructorParameterList = "(" (Parameter; ",")* ")";

// === Collection expression (C# 12.0, T3.12.2). ===
// A collection expression creates a collection from its elements:
//   var list = [1, 2, 3];
//   var empty = [];
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable):
//   * ParseCollectionExpression (Parser/LanguageParser.cs:13264-13291): `openBracket =
//     EatToken(OpenBracketToken)`, then ParseCommaSeparatedSyntaxList(IsPossibleCollectionElement,
//     ParseCollectionElement, allowTrailingSeparator:true, requireOneElement:false), then
//     `closeBracket = EatToken(CloseBracketToken)`. Returns CollectionExpression(openBracket, list,
//     closeBracket).
//   * IsPossibleCollectionElement (13293-13296) = IsPossibleExpression() — each element is an expression.
//     ParseCollectionElement (13298+) parses an expression / spread / with-element; the minimal task
//     models expressions only.
//   * Dispatch: ParsePrimaryExpressionWithoutPostfix `case SyntaxKind.OpenBracketToken`
//     (12023-12026) — a "[" in primary (expression) position is a collection expression (unless it is a
//     lambda). So a collection expression is a PRIMARY expression.
//   * CollectionExpressionSyntax (Generated/CSharpSyntaxGenerator/.../Syntax.xml.Main.Generated.cs:4193-4237):
//     OpenBracketToken + SeparatedSyntaxList<CollectionElementSyntax> Elements + CloseBracketToken.
//   * Version gate — collection expressions are a C# 12.0 feature (binder/semantic); the PARSER accepts
//     the form wherever the rule exists. Version purity comes from the rule being Cs12-only.
//
// T0.3 merge: re-declaring Primary APPENDS CollectionExpr to the Cs1 Primary (Cs1.grammar:719) — the same
// pattern as Cs11 RawStringLiteral (Cs11.grammar:43-45). A collection expression STARTS with "[", which no
// existing Primary alternative starts with (they start with a keyword / identifier / quote / digit / "("),
// so CollectionExpr is the SOLE match for a "[" in primary position -> no equal-length tie. The "[" of the
// PostfixOp Indexer (Cs1.grammar:776) is a POSTFIX op (requires a preceding primary), so it never competes
// with a primary-position "[".
//
// CS12 only: at v<=11 the CollectionExpr alternative is ABSENT, so `[1, 2, 3]` matches no Primary
// alternative and REJECTS.
//
// Boundary: the element list uses the default (Forbidden) trailing-separator behavior (like Cs1
// ArrayInitializer, Cs1.grammar:802), so `[1, 2, 3,]` (Roslyn allowTrailingSeparator:true) is rejected — a
// minor deviation, acceptable for the minimal task. Spread (`...x`) and with-element (`with(...)`)
// collection elements are out of scope (expressions only).
Primary =
    | CollectionExpr = "[" (Expression; ",")* "]";
